Article 62

1. For the prevention of acts of violence against women, the Regional Government shall carry out empowerment and awareness to families and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

JDIH Kota Singkawang (2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows:
a. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
b. opening employment opportunities for women;
c. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women from acts of violence;
d. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women; andf. opening complaint posts for the protection of women from acts of violence.

Part Seven Protection in the Workplace

Part Three

Prevention and Protection Article 22 (1) The Regional Government is obliged to make efforts to prevent and protect against acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment, and neglect.

(2) Implementation of prevention and protection as referred to in paragraph (1), may cooperate with educational institutions, religious institutions, mass media, professional organizations, business entities, and/or community institutions.

3. Prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (2) are implemented by:a. establishing networks;
b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of prevention and protection based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a system for prevention of violence and protection of children and women;

d. disseminating Laws and Regulations related to the protection of women victims of violence;
e. providing education for victims of violence and vulnerable groups;

JDIH Kota Singkawang
f. organizing family support in the form of family parenting education, child parenting education, family mediation, and economic support;
g. increasing the capacity of protection services for women and children including institutional capacity development and increasing the professionalism and competence of service providers;
h. increasing the ability of vulnerable individuals and/or groups to recognize risks and dangers from situations or acts that may cause violence, exploitation, maltreatment and neglect; and i. increasing the utilization of communication, information, and education media.
4. Prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the Regional Apparatus in charge of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 66

(1) Handling targets include:
a. children outside the care of parents;
b. children in disaster emergency situations;
c. children in conflict with the law;

d. child victims of physical, mental and sexual violence;
e. children subjected to maltreatment and neglect;
f. child victims of economic and sexual exploitation;
g. children who become victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse or things that cause dependence on narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances;
h. child victims of pornography;
i. children who have deviant social behavior;

j. children who experience stigmatization due to their parents' behavior;
k. children and women who become victims of human trafficking;

l. children and women who are in situations where the nature and circumstances of the workplace can endanger health, safety and morals; andm. women victims of physical, mental and sexual violence.
(2) Handling as referred to in paragraph (1) must be carried out immediately including:a. identification process and receipt of complaints/reports;
b. rescue action;
c. placement of children and women in temporary shelters;
d. rehabilitation including: JDIH Kota Singkawang
1. health recovery services;
2. psychosocial recovery services; and
3. legal assistance;
e. social reintegration.

Article 41

The Regional Government, community and other state institutions are obliged and responsible to provide special protection to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
JDIH Kota Singkawang
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immune deficiency syndrome;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;

j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorism networks;

l. children with special needs;
m. child victims of abuse and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

CHAPTER XVI

PROHIBITIONS

Article 98

Every person is prohibited from:
a. receiving child visitors and employing children in discotheque businesses, nightclubs, bars, karaoke, pubs/music houses, massage parlors and steam baths/saunas, and/or other worst forms of child labor places.
b. producing, selling and/or promoting cigarettes in school environments.
c. selling and/or promoting cigarettes, gambling equipment, contraceptives and goods that can damage the morals of children.
d. broadcasting and showing pornographic acts in community activities that can be accessed by children.
e. receiving child visitors during school hours or allowing child visitors after nine o'clock at night to access the internet at internet cafes.
f. refusing complaints and/or charging fees in providing services to victims at Protection Service Institutions.
g. treating children in a discriminatory manner that results in children experiencing losses both material and moral thereby hindering their social function;

h. treating children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
i. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of abuse and neglect;

j. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing violence against children;
k. committing violence or threat of violence forcing a child to have sexual intercourse with him or with another person;
l. committing violence or threat of violence, forcing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading a child to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
m. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking of children;

JDIH Kota Singkawang n. obstructing children from enjoying their own culture, recognizing and practicing the teachings of their religion and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;
o. recruiting or exploiting children for military and/or other interests, and leaving children without protection of life;

p. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit, or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of children;
q. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances; and r. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF CHILDREN, WOMEN, AND VICTIMS

Article 2

(1) Every child has the right:
a. to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth, and to receive protection from criminal acts, violence, exploitation, and neglect;
b. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. to worship according to their religion under the guidance of parents and to practice the teachings of their religion;

d. to know their parents, to be raised, and cared for by their own parents;

e. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
f. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with age level, physical and mental condition, intelligence and interests, and talents;
g. to express and be heard their opinions and to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with the level of intelligence and age for self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
h. to rest and utilize leisure time, play, express themselves for self-development;
i. to receive proper care from parents/guardians or other parties responsible for their care;

JDIH Kota Singkawang j. to obtain protection from abuse in politics, involvement in armed disputes, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of violence, involvement in warfare, targets of persecution, torture or imposition of inhuman punishment and involvement of children in the worst forms of work;
k. to obtain protection from the dangers of narcotics, pornography, and violent shows or other things that have an impact on changes in child growth and development;

l. to have contact with parents if separated; and
m. to obtain other rights in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
2. Every woman has the right:a. to live, maintain life, and improve living standards;
b. to live peacefully, safely, peacefully, happily, prosperously physically and mentally;
c. to enjoy a good and healthy living environment;

d. to live in a family within a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations;

e. to obtain work according to ability, conditions and fair and just wages;
f. specifically for women with disabilities, the right to receive facilities and special treatment in public services;
g. to play an active role in the field of politics and government in accordance with the potential and capabilities they possess;
h. to obtain legal information and services; and i. to obtain other rights in accordance with human dignity and based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. Every victim has the right to receive:a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;

d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. assistance in accordance with applicable provisions;

JDIH Kota Singkawang
g. guarantees of rights related to the victim's status as a family member and community member;

h. to obtain restitution in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
i. other rights regulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 3

Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 2 paragraph (1), and paragraph (3) also receive special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth, and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;

d. the right to be free from various stigma; and e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 70

(1) Health recovery services as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter d number 1 include: a. emergency services;
b. services for providing visum et repertum or visum psychiatrikum in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. follow-up services in the form of outpatient and/or inpatient care in accordance with medical provisions;
d. deoxyribose nucleic acid verification services;
e. delivery process services for pregnancy as a result of sexual violence and/or rape;
f. provision of further referral services in accordance with the victim's circumstances and condition; andg. provision of special rooms separate from general patients.
2. The OPD responsible for health affairs is obliged to provide health recovery services.

CHAPTER X

INSTITUTIONAL FRAMEWORK

Article 79

(1) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is the entire process of organizing women's empowerment and child protection carried out comprehensively, inclusively and integratively starting from the stage of report/complaint handling services, health services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance up to repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of crime and violence in their social environment.

2. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System aims to:a. improve the quality of life of women, children and the quality of families;
b. increase the institutional capacity of women's empowerment and child protection including the development of gender and child data; andc. provide special protection for children from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment.
3. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is carried out through:a. providing access for women and children to education, health and other strategic services;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience;

JDIH Kota Singkawang
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, socio-cultural and other strategic fields;
e. establishment, development and strengthening of the capacity of Women and Child Protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children as well as legal aid services;
f. improving the quality of human resources of managers;
g. strengthening the institutional capacity of regional gender and child mainstreaming;
h. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems; and
I. carrying out promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
4. In order to achieve comprehensive, inclusive and integrative women's empowerment and child protection in the region, the Regional Government shall establish:a. P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles;
b. regional child protection commission or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region; andc. other institutions according to needs and/or provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 11

(1) The obligations and responsibilities of parents as referred to in Article 7 include:
a. caring for, maintaining, educating, providing a sense of security and not committing mistreatment and violence against children;
b. developing children in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. reporting every birth of a child to the agency authorized to register births;
d. preventing marriage at child age; and
e. providing character education and instilling moral values in children.

(2) In the event that parents are absent, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family, which shall be carried out in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
(3) In the event that the parents and family of the child cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be transferred to a person or legal entity that meets the requirements appointed as the guardian of the child concerned.

